<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Meissen</h1><p class="st-meta">968 CE – 1581 CE · capital Meissen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ottonian bishopric among the Sorbs of the middle Elbe whose territory was gradually absorbed by the Wettin rulers of Saxony.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Otto I founded the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> in 968 as part of his reorganisation of the eastern frontier under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. It served the conversion of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sorbs">Sorbs</a> and the Slavic lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobr">Bóbr</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> shared the castle hill of Meissen with the margraves of Meissen.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> held scattered lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stolpen">Stolpen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzen">Wurzen</a> and Mügeln and governed a diocese that reached into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>. The Gothic cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, begun around 1260, rose beside the margraves' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrechtsburg">Albrechtsburg</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benno-of-meissen">Benno of Meissen</a>, bishop in the late 11th century, was canonised in 1523; his relics were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettin</a> dukes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> steadily controlled the see. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> became Lutheran, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ix-von-haugwitz">Johann IX von Haugwitz</a> resigned in 1581 and the territory was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a>. A Catholic remnant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> continued at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and became the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocese-of-dresden-meissen">Diocese of Dresden-Meissen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-meissen.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Meissen" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: Brunswyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dresden_Hofkirche_abends_(2005).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>968 CE – 1581 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Meissen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Benno of Meissen, Johann IX von Haugwitz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle High German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Meißen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1274&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-meissen" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Meissen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4986 405 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Meissen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5306 661L5229 694M5306 661L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5306 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5306 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Meissen. <a href="/atlas?year=1274&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-meissen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
